    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.\n\nIn both Cython and Python code, it's common to mark functions that do not return anything but may raise exceptions as returning C `int` for efficiency reasons.\n```cython\ncdef int func() except -1:\n    ...\n\n@cython.cfunc\ndef func() -> cython.int\n    ...\n```\nThis is both implicit and easily misleading because the signature cannot be distinguished from one that actually returns a relevant C `int` value. It also requires users to write `return 0` instead of just `return` (not sure if we should allow this, but it is an annoyance).\n\n### Describe the solution you'd like.\n\nI propose to add an explicit error return type to Cython so that users can write\n```cython\n@cython.cfunc\ndef func() -> cython.error_return\n```\nCython would handle this in the same way as `int except -1`.\n\nWe should probably allow this in both Cython and Python code, although we'd need to prevent naming conflicts in Cython code when we add a new basic type.\n\nWe could also normalise the return value to 0 / -1 to prevent users from accidentally passing meaningful integer values.\n\n### Describe alternatives you've considered.\n\nAlternative names:\n```cython\n@cython.func\ndef func() -> cython.raising\n```\nOther ideas?\n\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
    "closed_at": null,
    "closed_by": null,
    "comment_data": [
