        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> I think we could argue for \"optimising\" `def func() -> None` into a function that returns something else (an `int` error code) that is just used for signaling exceptions. Function pointers are an issue with that, though, as are .pxd exported functions. They would change their signature depending on the Cython version used, and that would be user visible, including for existing packages. `-> None` has been available since Cython 3.2.0, so there's probably code out there by now that uses it.\n\nFair enough yes it makes sense. I have one question though: Is there any performance benefit to \"optimize\" `def func() -> None` into C int return value? I am not expert in python C-API but when I studied the code, returning `None` it the only overhead is `__Pyx_INCREF` and `__Pyx_XDECREF`:\n\n```c\n  /* \"a.py\":29                                  \n * @cython.cfunc                                \n * def meh() -> None:                           \n *     return             # <<<<<<<<<<<<<<      \n *·                                             \n *·                                             \n*/                                              \n  {                                             \n    PyObject *__pyx_temp;                       \n    {                                           \n      __pyx_temp = __pyx_r;                     \n      __pyx_r = Py_None; __Pyx_INCREF(Py_None); \n    }                                           \n    __Pyx_XDECREF(__pyx_temp);                  \n  }                                             \n  goto __pyx_L0; \n``` \n\nThe exception check is also pretty cheap: ` __pyx_t_1 = __pyx_f_1a_meh(); if (unlikely(!__pyx_t_1)) __PYX_ERR(0, 33, __pyx_L1_error)`\n\n\nAm I something missing? Maybe yes. Based on AI discussion `__Pyx_INCREF` should be pretty cheap but it can be totally avoided in modern python since `Py_None` is immortal object...",
